From b87048e587acd132dca4b44e8cdf5c4bae2cc848 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Cl=C3=A9ment=20Fiere?= <clement@fiere.fr>
Date: Tue, 22 Sep 2026 23:28:44 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 11/23] fix(plugins): guard the registration's own label and
 icon

Round 3 filed four findings; this is the host-side delta.

- [BLOCKER] the new test file did not typecheck: the helper's `items` field
  was typed as the contract, so the two deliberately out-of-contract
  registrations (a promise, an unknown-returning wrapper) failed
  `tsc --noEmit`, which is a required apps/web CI step. The field is widened
  to `=> unknown` with one cast at the registration, matching what a
  JavaScript bundle can actually send.
- the guard stopped at `items()`: an action whose own `label` was an object or
  blank still reached React as a child, and an object `icon` was coerced into
  a key by the icon map, so an object with a throwing `toString` took the
  card's render down. `readActionLabel` omits such an action (reported, one
  entry fewer rather than a crash) and `readActionIcon` reads the icon once
  and falls back to no icon; `lookupPluginIcon` now maps only strings to
  names, which covers every icon surface (nav, panels, drawers, integrations)
  and makes an object icon the fallback glyph instead of a key lookup.
- tests: blank/object label (omitted, reported), an object icon that refuses
  coercion (entry kept, no throw), every processing flag disabling the plugin
  entries on both the prebuilt and internal paths, a hostile Array subclass
  proving the result is copied rather than called, and the two new child
  filter cases (blank label, non-boolean `disabled`).

Residuals documented rather than fixed, with reasons in the code: a throwing
*getter* on the registration object escapes before this module sees it (the
registry spreads the raw object into its own copy -- that boundary is the
registry's, for every registration type), and the once-per-action-and-kind log
set is never cleared, so the same defect kind recurring after a
re-registration stays silent for the page's life (clearing it would need a
registry hook that imports back into this module).
