From 98ce2665fab2c1ea5d01fd38c242877f8be81804 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Cl=C3=A9ment=20Fiere?= <clement@fiere.fr>
Date: Wed, 23 Sep 2026 06:23:57 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 19/23] fix(plugins): make the key escape injective
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Round 11 found two collisions the previous encoder could not prevent, both
reachable with ordinary ids:

- variable-width hex: a raw U+25E9 and the three characters `%E9` both
  encoded to `%25E9`, because a code unit under 0x100 emitted two hex digits
  (`%E9`) while a larger one emitted four (`%25E9`);
- a `%`-shaped separator: every escape starts with `%`, so plugin `p` with
  action `E9é` and plugin `pé` with action `E9` both produced
  `plugin-primary-p%E9%E9` -- the separator was indistinguishable from an
  escape.

The encoder escapes only the three characters a menu key gives meaning to --
`%` first, so an escape already inside an id cannot alias one this adds, then
`:`, then `#` -- and leaves everything else verbatim. That is injective and
delimiter-free by construction (every `%` in the result starts one of exactly
three escapes, so decoding is unambiguous, and neither `:` nor `#` survives raw
in a part), it cannot throw on a lone surrogate the way `encodeURIComponent`
does, and it keeps ids readable instead of opaque. The part separator is `:`
now, and every key expectation in the menu, command, edit and facet test files
follows it.

Tests added for both reported pairs (`U+25E9` versus `%E9`, and the two-plugin
`p`/`pé` pair), plus the existing lone-surrogate, `#`-bearing and child/action
cases, all asserting distinctness rather than a single spelling.
