From cff1423f02593ea6fe610421502f697956809183 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Cl=C3=A9ment=20Fiere?= <clement@fiere.fr>
Date: Wed, 23 Sep 2026 07:04:53 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 23/23] fix(plugins): make the icon guard total and the
 registry read defensive

Round 7 filed three MAJORs, all about a plugin object throwing where no
boundary exists.

- `isPluginIconComponent` accepted a `forward_ref` whose `render` was a class:
  `typeof render === "function"` is true for a constructor, and React then
  throws `Class constructor X cannot be invoked without 'new'` inside whatever
  surface renders the icon. Component-shaped functions are now checked to be
  callable (a class source is rejected), for the plain-function case too.
- the guard was not total: it read `$$typeof`, `render` and `type` outside any
  try, and `memo` recursion had no cycle guard, so `resolvePluginIcon({
  get $$typeof() { throw } })` and a self-referential memo both threw through
  callers that have no error boundary (nav, panels, drawers, integrations).
  The walk is now total -- one try per level, a depth bound -- so every
  malformed value answers "no icon" and falls back to the glyph.
- `pluginRegistry.getTaskMenuActions` spread each raw registration into a new
  object before the task-menu guards could see it, so a throwing getter on a
  registration escaped from the registry itself, during a card's render.
  Reading a registration now happens inside a per-entry catch: a malformed one
  is dropped and logged, and the rest of the list is unaffected.

Tests: a class payload, a hostile `$$typeof` getter, a cyclic memo wrapper,
nested real wrappers, and a registry entry whose getter throws alongside a
good one.
